# Let the session make an input device of its own.
#
# The daemon that reads the pad publishes a pointer through /dev/uinput -- that
# is how a thumb on the stick moves the cursor and how the on-screen keyboard
# types -- and it cannot start without one. The node belongs to root and the
# input group, so on a machine where the person is not in that group the daemon
# exits the moment it starts, which takes the keyboard with it: every key on
# this desktop is a bind that same process hands to the compositor.
#
# uaccess is the usual answer and it does not work for this one. It hands a
# seat's devices to whoever is logged in, and /dev/uinput belongs to no seat --
# it is a misc device, and the builtin finds no seat to ask about. The tag is set
# anyway, for the day that changes, and the permission is granted outright
# beside it, which is the same pair and the same reason as the controller's
# touchpad next door. `@user@` is the mark the manifest writes for whoever the
# desktop belongs to.
#
# This acts on the add event, so the module has to be loaded for there to be
# one: /etc/modules-load.d/console-uinput.conf is that, and without it the node
# is the static one udev makes from modules.devname, which carries the mode and
# the group and never runs a rule.
KERNEL=="uinput", SUBSYSTEM=="misc", OPTIONS+="static_node=uinput", \
  TAG+="uaccess", GROUP="input", MODE="0660", \
  RUN+="/usr/bin/setfacl -m u:@user@:rw /dev/uinput"
